// Dark Mode — replaces shadcn's per-framework dark-mode/*.mdx (next, vite,
// astro, remix, tanstack) with the one approach that applies to Marko 6.
// Code here is transcribed from this site's own src/routes/+layout.marko and
// src/tags/theme-toggle.marko — it is running on the page you are reading.
import "../../../tags/docs/docs-article.marko";
import "../../../tags/docs/code-block.marko";

static const TOC = [
  { id: "how-it-works", label: "How It Works" },
  { id: "blocking-script", label: "The Blocking Script" },
  { id: "toggle", label: "The Toggle" },
  { id: "system-only", label: "System Preference Only" },
  { id: "notes", label: "Notes" },
];

static const VARIANT = `@custom-variant dark (&:is(.dark *));`;

static const BLOCKING_SCRIPT = `<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>my-app</title>
    <html-script>
      (function () {
        var storedTheme = null;
        try {
          storedTheme = localStorage.getItem("theme");
        } catch (error) {}
        var prefersDark = window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches;
        var isDark = storedTheme ? storedTheme === "dark" : prefersDark;
        document.documentElement.classList.toggle("dark", isDark);
      })();
    </html-script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <\${input.content}/>
  </body>
</html>`;

static const TOGGLE = `<let/isDark=false/>

<script>
  isDark = document.documentElement.classList.contains("dark");
</script>

<button
  type="button"
  aria-label="Toggle theme"
  class="inline-flex size-8 items-center justify-center rounded-md text-foreground hover:bg-accent hover:text-accent-foreground"
  onClick() {
    const nextIsDark = !document.documentElement.classList.contains("dark");
    document.documentElement.classList.toggle("dark", nextIsDark);
    try {
      localStorage.setItem("theme", nextIsDark ? "dark" : "light");
    } catch (error) {}
    isDark = nextIsDark;
  }
>
  <if=isDark>Light mode</if>
  <else>Dark mode</else>
</button>`;

static const SYSTEM_ONLY = `@custom-variant dark (&:where(.dark, .dark *), @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark));`;

<docs-article
  title="Dark Mode"
  description="Toggle between light and dark themes by putting a .dark class on the root element, with no flash of the wrong theme on first paint."
  href="/docs/dark-mode"
  toc=TOC
>
  <p>
    marko-ui has no theme provider and no context. Dark mode is a single class on
    <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, because every token in the stylesheet is redefined under a
    <code>.dark</code> selector. Anything that reads those tokens — which is every component in the
    registry — follows automatically.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-it-works">How It Works</h2>

  <p>
    The stylesheet you install from the registry declares the Tailwind v4 dark variant explicitly:
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" filename="src/styles/globals.css" code=VARIANT/>

  <p>
    So <code>dark:bg-muted</code> applies whenever an ancestor carries <code>.dark</code>. Your
    only job is putting that class on the document element and keeping it in sync with the user's
    choice.
  </p>

  <h2 id="blocking-script">The Blocking Script</h2>

  <p>
    Reading the stored preference has to happen <strong>before the first paint</strong>, or the
    page renders light and then snaps to dark — the flash-of-wrong-theme. That means a synchronous
    script in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, which is where Marko 6 has a specific gotcha.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>In Marko 6, a bare <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag is not an HTML script tag.</strong>
    It is a client effect: a block of code Marko runs in the browser and re-runs when the state it
    references changes. To emit a literal <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> element into your markup, use
    <code>&lt;html-script&gt;</code>.
  </p>

  <code-block language="marko" filename="src/routes/+layout.marko" code=BLOCKING_SCRIPT/>

  <p>The script does three things, in this order:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>Reads <code>localStorage.theme</code>, wrapped in a <code>try</code> because storage throws in private-mode and sandboxed contexts.</li>
    <li>Falls back to the OS preference via <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> when nothing is stored.</li>
    <li>Toggles <code>.dark</code> on <code>document.documentElement</code> synchronously, before the body renders.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Keep it inline. An external script file is fetched asynchronously and defeats the entire point.
  </p>

  <h2 id="toggle">The Toggle</h2>

  <p>
    The toggle flips the class, persists the choice, and mirrors the result into local state so the
    button can show the right icon. Note that it reads the current theme from the DOM rather than
    from state — the server has no idea which theme the user prefers, so state is initialized in a
    client effect after resumption.
  </p>

  <code-block language="marko" filename="src/tags/theme-toggle.marko" code=TOGGLE/>

  <p>
    The <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block near the top is a Marko client effect (see above): it
    runs in the browser once, after the component resumes, and seeds <code>isDark</code> from
    whatever the blocking script decided. Rendering the icon from server-side state instead would
    produce a mismatch on every dark-mode visitor.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the exact component in this site's header — the theme control at the top right of this
    page is running that code.
  </p>

  <h2 id="system-only">System Preference Only</h2>

  <p>
    If you do not want a toggle at all and would rather follow the OS setting, drop the script and
    the button entirely and widen the variant to include the media query:
  </p>

  <code-block language="css" code=SYSTEM_ONLY/>

  <p>
    This is simpler and has no flash by construction, at the cost of users being unable to override
    their system setting for your site.
  </p>

  <h2 id="notes">Notes</h2>

  <h3>Why not store the theme in a cookie?</h3>
  <p>
    You can, and it lets the server render the correct class in the initial HTML, removing the need
    for the blocking script. It costs a cookie on every request and makes the page uncacheable per
    theme. The inline script is the simpler default; the cookie approach is worth it if you are
    already reading cookies in a middleware.
  </p>

  <h3>Do components need a dark-mode prop?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Components reference semantic tokens only, so a component authored without a single
    <code>dark:</code> utility still themes correctly. The <code>dark:</code> variants that do
    appear in the registry are for cases where a surface needs a different opacity or elevation in
    dark mode, not a different color.
  </p>

  <h3>The theme flashes on route changes</h3>
  <p>
    If the class is being reset, the blocking script is probably in a nested layout that re-renders
    rather than in the root layout. It belongs in the single root <code>+layout.marko</code> that
    owns the <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> element.
  </p>
</docs-article>
